{"name":"Purple Comet","hex":"#6e6970","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"},{"term":"comet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small Solar System body consisting mainly of volatile ice, dust and particles of rock whose very eccentric solar orbit periodically brings it close enough to the Sun that the ice vaporises to form an atmosphere, or coma, which may be blown by the solar wind to produce a visible tail.","A celestial phenomenon with the appearance of such a body."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/comet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Comet","blocks":[{"text":"A comet is an icy, small Solar System body or interstellar object that warms and begins to release gases when passing close to the Sun, a process called outgassing. This produces an extended, gravitationally unbound atmosphere or coma surrounding the nucleus, and sometimes a tail of gas and dust gas blown out from the coma. These phenomena are due to the effects of solar radiation and the outstreaming solar wind plasma acting upon the nucleus of the comet. Comet nuclei range from a few hundred meters to tens of kilometers across and are composed of loose collections of ice, dust, and small rocky particles. The coma may be up to 15 times Earth's diameter, while the tail may stretch beyond one astronomical unit. If sufficiently close and bright, a comet may be seen from Earth without the aid of a telescope and can subtend an arc of up to 30° across the sky. Comets have been observed and recorded since ancient times by many cultures and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Comet_Hale-Bopp_1995O1.jpg/330px-Comet_Hale-Bopp_1995O1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comet","wikidata":"Q3559"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Anglo-Saxon_Chronicle_-_cometa_%28British_Library_Cotton_MS_Tiberius_A_VI%2C_folio_10v%29.jpg/330px-Anglo-Saxon_Chronicle_-_cometa_%28British_Library_Cotton_MS_Tiberius_A_VI%2C_folio_10v%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Anglo-Saxon_Chronicle_-_cometa_%28British_Library_Cotton_MS_Tiberius_A_VI%2C_folio_10v%29.jpg/330px-Anglo-Saxon_Chronicle_-_cometa_%28British_Library_Cotton_MS_Tiberius_A_VI%2C_folio_10v%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A comet was mentioned in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle and by the  Venerable Bede  in the year 729 CE.[13][14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnglo-Saxon_Chronicle_-_cometa_(British_Library_Cotton_MS_Tiberius_A_VI%2C_folio_10v).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Structure_of_a_comet.jpg/500px-Structure_of_a_comet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Structure_of_a_comet.jpg/1280px-Structure_of_a_comet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Structure_of_a_comet.jpg/500px-Structure_of_a_comet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of a comet","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStructure_of_a_comet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Introducing_Comet_Hartley_2.png/500px-Introducing_Comet_Hartley_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Introducing_Comet_Hartley_2.png/500px-Introducing_Comet_Hartley_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nucleus of 103P/Hartley as imaged during a spacecraft flyby. The nucleus is about 2 km in length.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIntroducing_Comet_Hartley_2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Comet_wild_2.jpg/500px-Comet_wild_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Comet_wild_2.jpg/500px-Comet_wild_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Comet 81P/Wild exhibits jets on light side and dark side, stark relief, and is dry.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComet_wild_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb